window.BIZ_PAPERS = (window.BIZ_PAPERS || []).concat([{"id": "ENGI1-SAM", "unit": 1, "key": "SAM", "series": "Sample assessment (AAQ)", "date": "Pearson sample assessment material", "title": "Pearson sample paper", "source": "pearson", "kind": "exam", "code": "Sample", "spec": "new", "total": 90, "minutes": 135, "items": [{"q": 1, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "State the unit of measure for mechanical work.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correctly given unit of measurement from:\n• Joule\n• J\nAccept Joule/J with SI prefixes, e.g. kJ or Kilojoule.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "State the unit of measure for the modulus of rigidity.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correctly given unit of measurement from:\n• Pascal\n• Pa\n• Newtons per metre squared\n• N/m2\nAccept Pascal/Pa/N/m2 with SI prefixes, e.g. kPa or Kilopascal.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "The diagram shows a compound gear train. The driver gear is rotated at 300 revs/min.\n10 teeth\n6 teeth\n18 teeth\ndriver\ndriven\nDiagram not to scale\nCalculate the velocity of the driven gear.\nShow your working\nVelocity: rev/min", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer:\n• Output – 166.67 rev/min\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for recognising the need to calculate the\nvelocity ratio.\no VR = driven/driver\no VR = 10/18\no VR = 0.56\n• award one mark for rearranging in terms of output.\no VR = driven/driver\no driven = driver x VR\n• award one mark for correctly calculating the output velocity\nusing their values.\no Output = input x VR\no Output = 300 x 0.56 (ecf)\no Output = 166.67 revs/min (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "a", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": ["A car accelerates at a constant rate from 0m/s to a velocity (v) of 25 m/s in a time (t) of 9 seconds."], "text": "Calculate the rate of acceleration (a) of the car.\nYou should apply the formula v = u + at.", "ms": {"body": "Award two marks for the correct answer:\n• a = 2.78 m/s2\nIf the answer is incorrect:\naward one mark for correctly rearranging the formula to make\n‘a’ the subject\no 𝑎𝑎= (𝑣𝑣−𝑢𝑢)/t\n• Award one mark (FT) for the calculation of ‘a’ using an\nincorrectly rearranged formula", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": ["Show your working Acceleration:  m/s2"], "text": "The car then decelerates from 25m/s (u) to a velocity (v) of 13 m/s in a time (t) of\n6 seconds.\nCalculate the distance (s) travelled by the car when decelerating.", "ms": {"body": "Award two marks for the correct answer:\n• s = 114 m\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for the correct selection of formula to\ncalculate ‘s’ from the formula booklet\no 𝑠𝑠=\n1\n2 (𝑢𝑢+ 𝑣𝑣)𝑡𝑡", "levels": null}}, {"q": 5, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "An aeroplane travels north at a velocity of 220 m/s. The wind blows the aircraft to the\neast at a velocity of 30 m/s.\nDraw a vector diagram of the two velocities and the resultant velocity of\nthe aeroplane.\nYou should draw your diagram to an appropriate scale.\nN\nS\nE\nW", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for accurate plotting of velocity vector (220 m/s\nNorth)\nAward one mark for accurate plotting of wind vector (30 m/s East)\nAward one mark for accurate plotting of resultant velocity\n(allow ft)\nNose to tail version\nTail to tail version", "levels": null}}, {"q": 6, "sub": "a", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": ["A ring spanner is used to tighten a 12 mm nut with a constant force (f) of 9 N. The distance (d) from the centre of the nut to where the force is applied is 350 mm. 350 mm 12 mm 9 N Diagram not to scale"], "text": "Calculate the torque on the nut.", "ms": {"body": "Award two marks for the correct answer:\n• T = 3.15 Nm\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for correctly completing the unit conversion\nfrom mm to m.\no d = 350 mm = 0.35 m\n• award one mark for calculating torque using own figure for\nthe conversion of mm to m.\no T = 9 x 0.35 (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 6, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": ["Show your working Torque  Nm A different spanner with a length of 0.45 m is used to increase the torque. The same 9 N force (f) is applied."], "text": "Calculate the mechanical work done in rotating the spanner through one full\ncircle.", "ms": {"body": "Award two marks for the correct answer:\n• W = 25.45 J\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for correctly recognising that the angle θ\nthrough which the spanner is turned is 2π\no θ = 2π\no s = 2πr\no s = 2 x 0.45 x π (ecf)\no s = 2.878m (ecf)\n• Award one mark for calculation of work done by using the\nformula W = Fs\no W = Fs\no W = 9 x 2.878 (ecf)\no W = 25.45 J (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 7, "sub": "", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "The diagram shows a storage tank that contains a coolant that has a density of\n890 kg/m3.\nThe tank has a hinged door to allow the contents of the tank to be released.\n0.6 m\nCoolant\nHinge\nHinged\ndoor\nDiagram not to scale.\nThe door has an area of 0.8 m2.\nCalculate the overturning moment of the door around the hinge.\nShow your working\nOverturning moment Nm", "ms": {"body": "Award four marks for the correct answer:\n• Overturning moment = 628.625\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate the correct centre of pressure\no x = h/2\no x = 0.6/2\no x = 0.3\n• award one mark for recognition of the need to use relative\ndensity\no ρ = 890\n• award one mark for the correct process to calculate the\nhydrostatic thrust using F = ρgAx\no 𝐹𝐹= 𝜌𝜌g𝐴𝐴𝐴𝐴\no F = 890 x 9.81 x 0.8 x 0.3 (ecf/ft)\no F = 2095.42 N (ecf)\n• award one mark for the correct answer for the overturning\nmoment using students own values\no Moment = Fd = Fx\no Moment = 0.3 (ecf) x 2095.42 (ecf)\no Moment = 628.625 Nm", "levels": null}}, {"q": 8, "sub": "", "marks": 6, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "The diagram shows a cone with a radius (r) of 0.4 m and a height (h) of 1.2 m.\n0.4 m\n1.2 m\nDiagram not to scale\nThe cone floats in seawater with 50% of its volume submerged.\nCalculate the mass of the cone.\nAssume that:\n• the density (ρ) of the seawater is 1020 kg/m3\n• g = 9.81 m/s2\nShow your working\nMass kg", "ms": {"body": "Award six marks for the correct answer:\no Mass = 102.5 kg\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate the volume of the cone\no 𝑉𝑉=\n1\n3 𝜋𝜋𝑟𝑟2ℎ\no 𝑉𝑉= (𝜋𝜋0. 42 𝑥𝑥 1.2)/3\no 𝑉𝑉= 0.201 𝑚𝑚3\n• award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate the volume displaced\no Vd = 50% x 0.201 (ecf) = 0.1005 m3\n• award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate buoyant force/force upwards\no Fup = ρ x g x Vd\no Fup = 1020 x 9.81 x 0.1005 (ecf)\no Fup = 1005.6 N\n• award one mark for recognising that buoyant force equals\nweight\no F = ma\n• award one mark, for recognising acceleration = gravity\no a = g\n• award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate mass by rearranging 𝐹𝐹= 𝑀𝑀𝑀𝑀\no mass = Fup/g\no Mass = 1005.6(ecf)/9.81\no Mass = 102.5 kg", "levels": null}}, {"q": 9, "sub": "", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "A hammer with a mass of 3 kg is raised to a height of 4.5 m from the ground before\nbeing released.\nCalculate the velocity of the hammer as it hits the ground, assuming no air resistance.\n• Assume that g = 9.81 m/s2\nShow your working\nVelocity = m/s", "ms": {"body": "Award four marks for the correct answer:\no v = 9.4 m/s\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• Award one mark for recognising the need apply the concept\nof conservation of energy\no Potential Energy  = Kinetic Energy\no mgh = ½mv 2\no 132.4 = ½ x 3 x v2\n• Award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate potential energy\no PE = mgh\no PE = 3 x 9.81 x 4.5\no PE = 132.4 J\n• Award one mark for correctly rearranging in terms of v2\no v2 = 132.4 / (3 x ½ ) (ecf)\no v2 = 132.4/6 (ecf)\no v2 = 88.26 (ecf)\no v2 = 2gh\n• Award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate the value for v\no v = √ 88.26 (ecf)\no v = 9.4 m/s (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 10, "sub": "a", "marks": 3, "section": "A", "ctx": ["A punch is used to produce holes through sheet material."], "text": "The displacement of the punch (s) is given by the equation:\ns = t2 + 4t − 3\nDetermine the instantaneous velocity of the punch when t = 0.5 s.", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer: ds/dt = 5 m/s\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• Award one mark for setting up the differential for the\nexpression\n  - 𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑\n𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑= 2𝑡𝑡 + 4\n• Award one mark for differentiating with respect to t\n  - 𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑\n𝑑𝑑𝑑𝑑= 2 × 0.5 + 4\n• Award one mark for the answer for ds/dt or v\no V=ds/dt = 5 m/s", "levels": null}}, {"q": 10, "sub": "b", "marks": 5, "section": "A", "ctx": ["Show your working Velocity =  m/s"], "text": "The punch produces holes with a diameter of 0.2 m and the thickness of the sheet\nmaterial is 3 mm.\nForce (F)\n3 mm\nDiagram not to scale\nThe ultimate shear stress of the sheet material is 50 MPa.\nCalculate the force (F) needed to punch the hole through the sheet material.", "ms": {"body": "Award five marks for the correct answer: F = 94.25 kN\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• Award one mark for rearranging the formula 𝜏𝜏= 𝐹𝐹/𝐴𝐴 in\nterms of F\no F = τA\n• Award one mark for accurately converting mm to m\no 3mm = 0.003m\no 3mm = 3x10-3m\n• Award one mark for using the correct method to accurately\ncalculate the cutting area\no Cutting area = circumference x thickness\no A = 0.2 x π 0.003\no A = 0.2 x π x 3x10-3\no A = 1.88 x 10-3\n• Award one mark for accurately converting of MPa to Pa for τ\no 50 MPa = 50 000 000 Pa\no Or 50 MPa = 50x106 Pa\n• Award one mark for the calculation of F using own figures\no F = 50 000 000 x 1.88 x 10-3 (ecf)\no F = 94.25 kN (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 11, "sub": "", "marks": 7, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "A car of mass (m) 1250 kg accelerates up a road from an initial velocity (u) of 3 m/s to\na velocity (v) of 12 m/s.\nThe road is at an angle of 30° to the horizontal and has a coefficient of friction (μ)\nbetween the surface and the tyres of 0.88.\nThe car travels a distance (d) of 120 m.\nm = 1250 kg\n30°\nCalculate the average power produced by the car while it accelerates up the road.\nShow your working\nAnswer: W", "ms": {"body": "Award seven marks for the correct answer:\n• Average Power = 87450 W\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for the correct process to calculate the\ncomponent of the force due to the weight of the car that is\nparallel to the road surface\no Fp = mgsinθ\no Fp = 1250 x 9.81 x sin30\no Fp = 1250 x 9.81 x 0.5\no Fp = 6131.25 N\n• award one mark for recognition that the normal reaction\nforce is the same as the force parallel to the road as it is an\nalternate interior angle\no Fn=  mgcosθ\no Fn=  1250 x 9.81 x cos30\no Fn=  1250 x 9.81 x 0.87\no Fn=  10619.64\n• award one mark for the correct process to calculate the\nforce needed to overcome friction\no Fr=  μFn\no Fr=  0.88 x 10619.64 (ecf)\no Fr=  9345.3 N\n• award one mark for the correct process to calculate the\nacceleration of the car up the road\no a = (v2-u2)/2s\no a = (122-32)/2x120\no a = (144-9)/240\no a = 9/16\no a = 0.56 m/s2\n• award one mark for the correct process to calculate the\nwork done by the car to accelerate up the road\no WD = 120 x (Fp + ma + Fr)\no WD = 120 x (6131.25 + 1250x0.56 + 9345.3) (ecf)\no WD = 120 x (6131.25 + 700 + 9345.3)\n  - WD = 120 x 16176.55\n  - WD = 1941 kW\n• award one mark for the correct process to calculate the\ntime taken to accelerate up the road\no t = (v-u)/a\no t = (12-3)/0.56\no t = 9/0.56\no t = 16.1 s\n54\n• award one mark for the correct answer for average power\nproduced using students own values\no Power = WD/t\no Power = 1941(ecf) /16.1 (ecf)\no Power = 120.6 kW\n55\nSection B", "levels": null}}, {"q": 12, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "State the unit of measure for reactance.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correctly given unit of measurement from:\n• Ohms\n• Ω\n• Accept Ohm/ Ω with SI prefixes, e.g. kΩ or Kiloohm", "levels": null}}, {"q": 13, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "State the unit of measure for reluctance.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correctly given unit of measurement from:\n• Henry-1\n• H-1", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "a", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": ["The diagram shows a waveform for a sinusoidal voltage supply. Voltage (V) time (s) 0.005 0.015 0.025 0.035 0.01 0.02 0.03 0.04 −1 −2 −3"], "text": "Determine the amplitude of the voltage.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct answer:\n• Amplitude = 2.5 V", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "b", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Determine the time period of the voltage.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the correct answer:\n• Time period = 0.02 s", "levels": null}}, {"q": 15, "sub": "a", "marks": 3, "section": "B", "ctx": ["The current of an exponentially decaying capacitor is given by the formula: I = I0 e−t/τ Where I is the current, I0 is the original current, t is the time (in seconds) and τ is the time constant. I0 = 0.6 A. When t = 0.1, I = 0.4 A"], "text": "Prove, using the laws of logarithms, that τ = 0.25", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each of the following correctly completed\nmathematical processes in the correct order, up to three marks\nfor:\n• rearranging the expression in terms of ‘e’\no 0.4 = 0.6 e-0.1/τ\no 0.667 = e-0.1/τ\n• recognition of the need to take the natural log (ln) on both\nsides of the expression\no ln0.667 = ln(e-0.1/τ )\no ln0.667 = -0.1/τ\n• rearranging in terms of τ\no τ = -0.1/ln0.667", "levels": null}}, {"q": 15, "sub": "b", "marks": 3, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Show your working The capacitor has two plates. Each has an area of 1.125 × 10−3 m2. The plates are separated by a layer of glass which has a thickness of 2 mm and a relative permittivity of 3.7."], "text": "Calculate the capacitance of the capacitor.", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer:\no C = 18.4x10-12 F\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for recognition of the need to use the\nrelative permittivity and permittivity of free space\no ε = ε0 x εr\no εr of glass = 3.7\no ε0 = 8.85 × 10−12 F/m\no ε0 = 3.7 x 8.85 × 10−12\no ε0 = 32.75 × 10−12\n• award one mark for conversion of mm to m\no d = 0.002\no d = 2 x 10-3\no C = 3.7 x 8.85 × 10−12 x 1.125x10-3 / 0.002\n• award one mark for the answer for capacitance\no C = 3.7 x 8.85 × 10−12 x 1.125x10-3 / 0.002 (ecf)\no C = 18.4x10-12 F (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 16, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A transformer has 180 secondary turns and is supplied by a 230 V RMS AC supply.\n230 volt\ninput\nsupply\nOutput\nsupply\nPrimary\ncoil\nSecondary\ncoil 180\nturns of wire\nSoft iron core\nA peak voltage of 84 V is required as the output from the transformer.\nCalculate the number of primary turns required on the transformer.\nShow your working", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer:\no 697 turns\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for recognition of the need to calculate RMS\nvoltage.\no Verm’s = Vpeak/√2\no Vrms = 84/√2\no Vrms = 59.4V\n• award one mark for rearranging in terms of Ns\no Vp/Np = Vs/Ns\no Np = Vp x Ns/Vs\no Np = 230 x 180 / 59.4\no Np = 696.97\n• award one mark for recognition that the number of turns\nmust be a whole number\no Np = 697 turns (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 17, "sub": "a", "marks": 4, "section": "B", "ctx": ["A coil with 200 turns has a current of 3 A flowing through it. The coil is wrapped around a circular bar with a cross-sectional area of 0.4 m2. The bar is 250 mm long. The relative permeability (μr) of the circular bar is 150 and the permeability of free space (μ0) is 4π × 10−7 H/m."], "text": "Prove that the flux density (B) = 0.452 T.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each of the following correctly completed\nmathematical processes in the correct order, up to four marks:\n• for substituting the expression for H into the formula to find\nB.\no B/(NI/ l) = μ0 μr\n• for rearranging in terms of B.\no B= μ0 μr (NI/ l)\n• for conversion of mm to m.\no 250mm = 0.25m\n• for the correct process to find the value for B.\no B = (4π×10−7 x 150 x 200 x 3) /0.25\no B = 0.452 T", "levels": null}}, {"q": 17, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Show your working"], "text": "Calculate the magnetic flux (Φ) in the circular bar.", "ms": {"body": "Award two marks for the correct answer: Φ = 0.181 Wb\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for the rearranging in terms of Φ\no Φ = BA\no Φ = 0.452 x 0.4\n• award one mark for F using students own rearranged\nformula\no Φ = 0.181 Wb", "levels": null}}, {"q": 18, "sub": "", "marks": 3, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "The diagram shows a resistor network.\nR1\nR2\n220 Ω\n560 Ω\nR3\n56 Ω\nR4\n33 Ω\nR5\n100 Ω\n9 V\nCalculate the total resistance of the resistor network.\nShow your working\nTotal Resistance: Ω", "ms": {"body": "Award three marks for the correct answer: R = 346.9 Ω\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for rearranging in terms of Rp (ft)\no 1/Rp = 1/R1 +1/R2\no 1/Rp = 1/560 + 1/220\no Rp = 1/0.00633\no Rp = 157.9 Ω\n• award one mark for recognition that the total resistance is\nthe sum of the three series resistances and the equivalent\nresistance\no R = Rp + R3 + R4 + R5\no R = Rp + 56 + 33 + 100\no R = 157.9 (ecf) + 56  + 33 + 100\n• award one mark for the answer for resistance using their\nvalues\no R = 346.9 Ω (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 19, "sub": "", "marks": 4, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A conductor is connected to a 12 V DC supply. A charge (Q) of 36 C flows along the\nconductor in a time (t) of 0.4 minutes.\nCalculate the resistance of the conductor in Ohms.\nShow your working\nResistance: Ω", "ms": {"body": "Award four marks for the correct answer:\n• R = 8 Ω\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• Award one mark for identifying the need to use Ohms Law:\no V = IR\n• award one mark for recognising the need to convert the\ntime into seconds\no 0.4 minutes = 24 seconds\n• award one mark for selecting the correct formula to\ncalculate I:\no I = q / t\no I = 36 / 24 (ecf)\no I = 1.5 A\n• award one mark for calculation of R using own figure for I:\no R = 12 / 1.5 (ft)\no R = 8", "levels": null}}, {"q": 20, "sub": "", "marks": 7, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A coil is connected to a 230 V AC power supply that has frequency of 50 Hz.\n560 Ω\n230 V\n50 Hz\nDetermine the inductance (L) of the coil if the supply has a current of 0.125 A.\nShow your working\nInductance (L) H", "ms": {"body": "Award seven marks for the correct answer:\no L = 5.58 H or L = 5.6 H\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for identifying the relationship between\nimpedance, reactance and resistance\no Z = √(XL2 + R2)\no Z2 = XL2 + R2\n• award one mark for recognition that to calculate reactance\nOhm’s law needs to be applied\no Z = V/I\no Z = 230/0.125\no Z = 1840 Ω\n• award one mark for rearranging in terms of XL2\no XL2 = Z2-R2\no XL2 = 18402 - 5602\no XL2 = 3072000\n• award one mark for taking the square root to determine XL\no XL = √3072000\no XL = 1752 (ecf)\n• award one mark for recognition of the need to calculate L\no XL = 2πfL\n• award one mark for rearranging in terms of L\no L = XL/2πf\no L = 1752/2π x 50 (ecf)\n• award one mark for the correct answer for L\no L = 5.58 H (ecf)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 21, "sub": "", "marks": 6, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A capacitor (C) is fully charged and the voltage across it is 1000 V.\nWhen the switch is moved to position B the capacitor discharges through the\nresistor R which has a resistance of 50 kΩ.\nThe initial discharge current is 0.02 A. The capacitor discharges a total of 250 J of\nenergy.\nA\n10 kΩ\n1000 V\nSwitch\nB\nC\nR\nCalculate the current flowing through the capacitor 1 second after it starts to\ndischarge.\nShow your working\nCurrent: A", "ms": {"body": "Award six marks for the correct answer:\no I = 0.019A\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for recognition that the relationship\nbetween electrical energy and capacitance is needed to\ncalculate the time constant\no W = ½CV2\n• award one mark for rearranging in terms of C\no C = 2W/V2\no C = 2x250/10002\no C = 0.0005F\no C = 500x10-6F\no C = 500µF\n• award one mark for recognition of the need for values to be\nin base units\no τ = 50 000 x 0.0005\no τ = 50 000 x 500x10-6F\n• award one mark for calculating the time constant\no τ = RC\no τ = 25s (ecf)\n• award one mark for the recognising the need to derive an\nexpression using the capacitor discharge formula and Ohm’s\nLaw\no 𝐼𝐼=\n𝑉𝑉\n𝑅𝑅= (𝑉𝑉𝑒𝑒൫−𝑡𝑡𝜏𝜏\nൗ൯)/𝑅𝑅\n• award one mark for the current using students own values\no 𝐼𝐼= (1000𝑒𝑒൫−1 25\nൗ\n൯)/50000\no I = 0.019 A\no I = 0.02 A", "levels": null}}, {"q": 22, "sub": "", "marks": 6, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "The diagram shows a resistor network connected to two DC power supplies.\n10 Ω\nR1\nR2\nR4\nR3\nV1\nl1\nl2\n6 Ω\n12 V\nV2\n9 V\n20 Ω\n22 Ω\nleft hand\nloop\nright hand\nloop\nCalculate the current flowing through resistor R3.\nShow your working\nCurrent: A", "ms": {"body": "Award six marks for the correct answer:\no I = 0.8A\nIf the answer is incorrect:\n• award one mark for setting up an equation for I3 in terms of\nI1 and I2\no I3 = I1 + I2\n• award one mark for producing an equation for the current I1\nin the left hand loop\no 12V = (10+2)I1 + 6(I1 + I2)\no 12V = 12I1 + 6(I1 + I2)\no 12V = 18I1 + 6I2\n• award one mark for producing an the current I2 in the right\nhand loop\no 9V = 22 I2 +6(I1 + I2)\no 9V = 28 I2+6 I1\no 6 I1 = 9V - 28 I2\n• award one mark for substitution into the equation for the LH\nloop\no 12V = 3(9V - 28 I2) + 6 I2\no 12V = 27V – 84 I2 + 6I I2\no 78I2 = 15V\no I2 = 0.19A\n• award one mark for substitution back into the equation to\ndetermine I1\no 18I1 = 12 – (6x0.19)\no 18I1 = 12 – 1.15\no 18 I1 = 10.85A\no I1 = 0.61A\n• award one mark calculating the value of I3\no I3 = I1 + I2\no I3 = 0.19A + 0.61A = 0.80A\n64\nSeptember 2026\nFor information about Pearson Qualifications, including Pearson Edexcel\nand BTEC qualifications visit qualifications.pearson.com\nEdexcel and BTEC are registered trademarks of Pearson Education Limited\nPearson Education Limited. Registered in England and Wales No. 872828\nRegistered Office: 80 Strand, London WC2R 0RL.\nVAT Reg No GB 278 537121", "levels": null}}], "badge": "Pearson · current spec", "badgeTone": "new", "notice": "Pearson’s sample assessment for the current AAQ specification: the closest match to the real exam. Figures, tables and information booklets are described in words."}]);
